// fontsrc.nv — the seam.  A place in a font file, said out loud, and
// the trait that lets a host fill it.
//
// Every body here is a `todo()`; the signatures are the design.
//
// THIS MODULE IS THE LOAD-BEARING INTERFACE OF THE PACKAGE.
//
// A font is not a document, it is an INDEX: twelve bytes say how many
// tables there are, sixteen bytes per table say where each one lives,
// and every table after that is independent of every other.  Drawing a
// line of English text reads four of them — `cmap` to turn characters
// into glyph ids, `hmtx` for the advances, `loca` and `glyf` for the
// outlines of the glyphs actually used — out of the twenty a modern
// font carries.  Noto Sans CJK is 20 MB and a terminal draws ASCII out
// of it; a web page's font arrives over a socket a chunk at a time.
// Reading the whole file first is a decision the caller should be able
// not to make.
//
// So this package never reads anything, and says where instead.  Every
// answer about WHERE something is, is a `FontRange`, and it means two
// things at once:
//
//   - to a caller holding the whole file, it is a SPAN into the buffer
//     it already has, and `fontsrc.slice` cuts it out;
//   - to a caller holding no file at all — a `File` with a `seek`, an
//     HTTP range request, a flash page — it is a REQUEST: read these
//     `len` bytes at `at` and hand them back.
//
// `docs/publishing.md` § How a `core` package takes bytes from its host
// calls the second one "the core asks, the host performs", and elf-nv
// is the package on the grid that answered the same shape first.
//
// WHY A TRAIT AND NOT JUST A RANGE.  elf-nv stops at the range and
// leaves the loop to the caller; a font's loop is longer than an ELF
// file's — open the directory, find `head`, read it to learn the `loca`
// format, read `loca` to find the glyph, read the glyph, and if it is a
// composite go round again — and written out by hand at every call site
// it is where the bugs live.  `FontSource[e]` lets that loop be written
// ONCE, in `fontread`, without the package acquiring an effect: the
// bound binds the trait's effect parameter and the clause is `[e]`, so
// `read_face` costs `[io, fs]` over a `File` and NOTHING over a buffer,
// and the package stays `core` either way (SPEC § 5.6, and
// `docs/publishing.md` § Design, third shape).
//
// WHY THE TRAIT IS THIS PACKAGE'S AND NOT `std.io`'s `Read` + `Seek`.
// Because the compiler will not take both: a function may bind exactly
// ONE effect parameter, and `<S: Read[e] + Seek[e]>` is `E3005` with a
// message saying a clause mixing two supplies could not say which name
// each filled.  That is the right refusal and not a defect — but it
// means a random-access source cannot be spelled with the standard
// library's two traits at all, so this package declares the one trait
// that is random access by construction: a range in, the bytes out.
// A host that has a `File` writes the four-line impl once.

use fonterror

// A place in a font file: `len` bytes starting at `at`.
//
// Offsets are counted from the first byte of the FILE, never from the
// start of the table being walked.  A caller that read one table out
// and is now parsing it holds that table's own `at` and adds it — it
// is the only party that knows it did that, and a range that sometimes
// meant one and sometimes the other would be a coin flip at every call
// site.
//
// Not `@value`: a range is what `fontdir.table_range` answers as an
// optional and what several functions carry inside a `Result`, and
// SPEC § 14.5 keeps an unboxed struct out of both positions.
pub struct FontRange
    // Byte offset of the first byte, from the start of the file.
    at: Int
    // How many bytes.  Zero is a legal answer — an empty `glyf` entry
    // is how a font spells "this glyph has no outline", which is what
    // a space is.
    len: Int

// The range of `len` bytes at `at`.
//
// ```novo
// use fontsrc
//
// fn main() [io]
//     let r = fontsrc.range(12, 16)
//     println("${r.len} bytes at ${r.at}")
// ```
pub fn range(at: Int, len: Int) -> FontRange []
    todo("font-nv.fontsrc.range")

// The zero-length range at `at`.
//
// Named rather than left to `range(at, 0)` because an empty glyph is
// the common case — every space, every unencoded slot — and a reader
// that meets one should see a word for it rather than a zero.
pub fn empty(at: Int) -> FontRange []
    todo("font-nv.fontsrc.empty")

// Whether the range covers no bytes at all.
pub fn is_empty(r: FontRange) -> Bool []
    todo("font-nv.fontsrc.is_empty")

// One past the last byte: `at + len`.
pub fn range_end(r: FontRange) -> Int []
    todo("font-nv.fontsrc.range_end")

// Whether the whole range lies inside a file of `total` bytes.
//
// The check every offset field gets before it is believed.  A table
// directory is a list of numbers some tool wrote, and nothing in the
// format stops one naming bytes past the end of the file; a caller
// doing its own reading asks this before it seeks.
pub fn range_fits(r: FontRange, total: Int) -> Bool []
    todo("font-nv.fontsrc.range_fits")

// A range inside another range, with `at` relative to the outer one.
//
// The arithmetic that turns "the `cmap` subtable at offset 28 of the
// `cmap` table" into a file offset, in one place rather than at every
// call site that does it.  The result is clipped to the outer range,
// so a nested offset that overruns answers a shorter range rather than
// one pointing outside the table.
pub fn sub(outer: FontRange, at: Int, len: Int) -> FontRange []
    todo("font-nv.fontsrc.sub")

// The range's bytes, cut out of a file the caller holds whole.
//
// The convenience half of the type, for the caller that DID read the
// file: `slice` and the trait below are the same fact reached from the
// two ends.  Answers a short slice rather than failing when the range
// overruns, because the caller's next parse will refuse with a
// `FontShort` that names the structure, which is the more useful
// message.
pub fn slice(file: Bytes, r: FontRange) -> Bytes []
    todo("font-nv.fontsrc.slice")

// Whether `have` bytes are enough for a structure needing `want`, and
// the fault to answer when they are not.
//
// Every parser in this package opens with this pair, so the shortfall
// is reported the same way from twelve places rather than twelve ways.
pub fn enough(have: Int, want: Int) -> Bool []
    todo("font-nv.fontsrc.enough")

// The `FontShort` for a table that ran out.
pub fn short(table: Int, want: Int, have: Int) -> fonterror.FontFault []
    todo("font-nv.fontsrc.short")

// ── The trait that crosses the layer ─────────────────────────────────

// A source of font bytes the host performs against.
//
// One method, and it is random access on purpose: a font is an index,
// and an index read sequentially is a file read whole.  An
// implementation over a `File` seeks and reads; one over a `Bytes`
// slices and costs nothing; one over an HTTP connection sends a
// `Range` header.
//
// The effect parameter is what makes the difference invisible to this
// package and visible to the caller: an impl declares what it performs,
// and every function bound to `FontSource[e]` is charged that and no
// more.
//
// ```novo ignore
// impl FontSource[io] for FontFile
//     fn font_bytes(self, r: FontRange) -> Result<Bytes, FontFault> [io]
//         match self.handle.seek(SeekStart(r.at))
//             Err(e) => Err(FontSourceFailed(e.message()))
//             Ok(_)  =>
//                 match self.handle.read(r.len)
//                     Err(e) => Err(FontSourceFailed(e.message()))
//                     Ok(b)  => Ok(b)
// ```
pub trait FontSource[e]
    // Answer exactly the bytes the range names.
    //
    // Short is an error, not a truncation: a parser handed nine bytes
    // where it asked for sixteen would read a field out of the next
    // table, and `FontSourceFailed` at the seam is a message a caller
    // can act on where a wrong advance width is not.
    fn font_bytes(self, r: FontRange) -> Result<Bytes, fonterror.FontFault> [e]
